  "text": "Over the last three weeks, I've been building this short form editing skill, and I've used it to create the last 12 premium reels that I posted on my Instagram. And one of them even hit 90,000 views. Now a lot of people have been wondering if these are edited using AI or an actual editor. So today I'm finally showing you how I trained Claude to edit my short form videos for Instagram. First, I'm gonna break down the five keys that make up my viral short form editing style so that you can understand it. Second, I'll walk you through the training process of how you can create a viral short form editor skill for any editing style, including mine. And finally, I'll take a raw short form video and edit it using this skill with you step by step. And by the end of the video, you'll know exactly how you can train Claude on any editing style that you want, and how to use it to edit viral short form videos like the ones you love. If you want access to this very skill, I just dropped the Joseph AI editing masterclass where I'm sharing the skills that edit both my short form and long form content. It's all in Ultimate Editors, and the link is down below. But without further ado, before we learn how we can create the skill that edits short form videos for us, first let's understand the style that we're about to train it on. You see, I've been posting for quite some time, and a lot of my audienfically loved the style of the videos that we were making. It felt clean, it was premium, and it was really simple. So my goal with this skill that I was about to build was very simple. I wanted to train Claude on how it can create these exact videos. So before we do that, let's first break down what my editing style is actually made of. The Joseph Shortform editing skill is made up of five keys. If you combine these five keys, you get premium stunning edits that look like this. The first key to the Joseph editing style is the captions. We start with our A roll and break down the scene into thirds. Then we add a darkness and blur to the lower third, and our caption sits in this box here. The caption is made up of two separate fonts. A simple sounds font for the regular text for using create or display, and a special script font for the highlighted text, where we use Apple Garmouth. When we place the text, we start by placing one or two script fonts for the special words, then we look for gaps around it where the rest can fit. And we fit the rest of the sentence in using the sounds font. Finally, for the text animation, it's a simple pop up blur fade in where the text pops up, fades in from low opacity, and even has a blur at the beginning as an extra touch. The first key is done, now onto the second key, the on-screen UI listles. A lot of our videos are chapter based, where we walk the viewer through chapter one, then two, then three. So we use these UI listals to show the viewer three chapters early in the video. For the design, it's a Rhombus with a slight gradient and a stroke around the outside that's thin, with a number that's in the middle. For the chapter animation, we simply introduce each one with a pop up fade. Then every time we mention the chapter throughout the video, we'll reuse it with text next to it to name the chapter. Key three is our on screen UI pops and number counters. There's many different kinds, and they all differ based on what is being said in the script. For example, when we talk about two elements or compare two things, we use a before and after layout like this. Object on the left, object on the right, linked with a clean simple air. When we mention a certain software or an event, we can use a UI pop up animation like this, with a blurred background, the logo of the software, and text representing the notification. If we ever mention timers or counters, we use a circular animation like this one. It has a gray stroke background and a colored foreground, and number in the middle that represents the amount. And if we ever need to list out multiple things, we pop up a blurred square just like this one and list out each element in a sequence like this. Key four is how we create engaging hooks. We use the simple three-piece formula for our hooks, speaker, text, and visual. The first thing we do is ensure the speaker is visible by aligning our speakers' eyes to the upper thirds of the video, we can ensure that the viewer instantly sees the speaker and connects with it. Then for the text, it all starts by looking at the script and asking ourselves what about this makes the viewer want to watch the video. Once you've found it, then you want to emphasize it using text. Finally, for the visuals, we simply ask ourselves what can we show on the screen that would support the text in hooking the viewer. This could be popping up the editing styles that we're talking about, or it could be show a timer if the video is about how short we're gonna be teaching the viewer a certain thing. Finally, key five is the full screen animations. These are the bread and butter of the video. They take up half of the edit, but have the most impact. These fully depend on what.",
